# Documentation Structure

> **Status:** Normative (Mandatory) · **Scope:** All Contributors & AI Agents

The file roles, their boundaries and the DRY rules are in the canon —
**[AGENTS.canon.md](../AGENTS.canon.md)** → "Files and their roles" and
"Documentation discipline". They are not repeated here.

This file says only where documentation goes **in this repository**.

## 1. Developer guidelines (`docs/development/`)

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `commit.md` | Project-specific commit scopes, if any. The commit standard itself is in the canon |

Development docs describe *process* — how to commit, how to test, how to
release. They never paraphrase source code, and never duplicate what
`pyproject.toml` or the `justfile` already states.

## 2. Overlay documentation (`docs/<overlay>/`)

If an overlay template is applied (expert report, thesis), it owns exactly one
directory under `docs/` — for example `docs/gutachten/` or `docs/thesis/` —
holding its binding domain rules and its own `AGENTS.md`.

Those files are **maintained upstream** in the overlay repository and pulled in
via `copier update`; edit them there, not here. Nothing outside that directory
duplicates their content.

The nested `AGENTS.md` follows the canon's rule: it may only narrow the root
`AGENTS.md`, the root file lists it, and it opens with its scope.

## 3. Scripts (`scripts/`)

Lifecycle and build scripts called from `justfile` recipes. Each script carries a
docstring saying what it does and which `just` command calls it.

## 4. Agent tooling

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `AGENTS.md` | Entry point — what applies in this repository |
| `AGENTS.canon.md` | Generated, byte-identical house rules. Never edited here |
| `.agent/workflows/*.md` | Workflow definitions for autonomous agents |

## 5. Generated and scrap material

Temporary notes, drafts and one-off audits go to `.tmp/`{% if not workspace_location_external %} or `_workspace/`{% endif %} —
both gitignored. They never enter the repository.
